{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-bc5ee04c-72b9-4f79-809b-f99fa110b37a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba428707223db18c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-76z2k0yq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n\n**Strategy**: AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training\n**Triage**: Not below-threshold — the strategy spans four components, two owning teams, and multiple distinct work streams (a hardware validation campaign, serving-runtime productization, training-stack enablement, and substantive documentation authoring). Not docs-only — three of four HLRs require code and image deliverables.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm serving runtime image | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorch ROCm training support for PyTorchJob single- and multi-GPU jobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue quota handling for the amd.com/gpu resource | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | Document AMD hardware support, driver prerequisites, and validated models | Implementation | RHOAI Documentation | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 validation campaign]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 ROCm serving runtime]\n    E003[RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 Konflux pipeline]\n    E004[RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 PyTorch ROCm training]\n    E005[RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota]\n    E006[RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 documentation]\n    E001 --> E002\n    E002 --> E006\n    E003 --> E006\n    E004 --> E006\n    E005 --> E006\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productized runtime must support — it changes the scope of the productization epic (true gate, `rewrite`). Recorded as `gated_by` with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation must state the validated model list and the supported runtime — it describes what was built. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs cover the released, pipeline-built runtime; E003 is an Implementation epic, so docs are blocked by it. |\n| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Driver prerequisites and training support statements require the training work to be final. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation of supported job/quota behavior requires the quota handling to be final. |\n| *no* E003 → E002 / E002 → E003 | Rule 10 (konflux ∥ general implementation) | The image-build epic and its Konflux pipeline onboarding run in parallel; the coupling is expressed as a \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on E002 (Rule 25), never as a DAG edge. |\n| *no* E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (decision rule) | The campaign outcome changes *which models* the runtime supports, not *whether a build pipeline for the ROCm image exists* — the pipeline is identical for every non-empty go scope, so it is not a gated epic. |\n| *no* E005 → E004 | Rule 13 (configuration references are AC gates, not edges) | The Kueue quota configuration is consumed by training jobs as a configuration reference; the end-to-end admission check is an acceptance criterion on E004, not a build-against dependency. |\n| *no* edges among E001, E003, E004, E005 | Rules 14/15 (independent work) | Different components/repos with no shared build artifacts — the campaign runs on upstream ROCm builds, the pipeline is repo-local config, and the training work is in separate repos. All are Tier 1 (see below). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware producing parity data and the go/no-go scope. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models the campaign clears; gated by E001. Build/release infrastructure (E003) runs in parallel per Rule 10. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E004, E005 | Split by component boundary (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads), not by priority — both halves are P1 and required for the same HLR. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E006 | `docs-authoring` priority exception: strategy frontmatter priority is Major → P1 (not the HLR's P2). Blocked by all Implementation epics per Rule 11. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n- **KServe** is listed as an affected component but the strategy describes no code change for it: the serving control plane is accelerator-agnostic and the ROCm stack lives in the runtime image. KServe is therefore classified as **passive**, owned by the same team (RHOAI Serving) as the nearest active component (Serving Runtimes) — so KServe-level validation (InferenceServices deploying the ROCm runtime, endpoint readiness) is an acceptance criterion on E002 rather than a separate epic.\n- **E006 component**: `Documentation` (canonical list entry). The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, so the owning team `RHOAI Documentation` is an assumption logged in Health Warnings.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Owning teams were inferred from the architecture-context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/*.md`); epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n2. **Docs team assumption**: `RHOAI Documentation` as the owner of E006 is inferred, not stated — verify with the strategy author.\n3. **Scope trap — amd.com/gpu resource exposure**: HLR-3 presumes the `amd.com/gpu` resource exists on nodes, but the mechanism that exposes it (AMD GPU device plugin / GPU Operator) is not in the platform inventory and is not addressed by the strategy (the architecture context lists only the NVIDIA GPU Operator for accelerator discovery). If productizing that mechanism turns out to be in scope, an additional epic under the Platform Operator component is needed — flagged for human verification rather than invented here.\n4. **Hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes are an availability precondition granted by parties outside the strategy (procurement / partner lab). Per the AC rules it is recorded as a risk on E001 (whose scope includes securing access), not as an acceptance criterion — the AC sits on the campaign's validated report.\n5. **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: an external dependency with uncertain timing, controlled by AMD and the upstream communities. Handled through tiered delivery (below) and an acceptance criterion on E002 (a released, platform-version-pinned ROCm image), not through a DAG edge.\n6. **Priority note**: E003 inherits P0 (it enables the P0 HLR-2 release path even though it carries no DAG edge to E002 — the coupling is the Rule 25 AC).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (AMD/upstream ROCm image cadence) and the campaign go/no-go both have uncertain timing; partial value is deliverable without them:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value / risk reduction)**: E001 (parity data and go/no-go scope, using upstream ROCm builds), E003 (build pipeline, known-recipe work needed for any go outcome), E004 and E005 (training enablement, independent of the serving go/no-go).\n- **Tier 2 (gated)**: E002 (productization, gated by the campaign outcome and effectively by availability of a releasable upstream ROCm base), E006 (documentation, always last).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32999,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cd51f53f58a6d98","agent_id":"ae6b38f613648449c","session_id":"ca871b20-94d5-42d0-a66d-2838b98e5410","uuid":"429c7f68-0eea-41f4-917a-b314fcb44143","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:24:58.065Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020 into epics"}
