{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-24cfa27f-8b0e-4888-91f7-66e44503d2df","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b29ce42a1e1cc3be","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-76z2k0yq/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM serving parity\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the first-hand functional and performance validation of vLLM serving on physical AMD Instinct accelerators, covering the platform's supported model set. It resolves the strategy's central unknown — whether ROCm builds of vLLM reach functional and performance parity with the productized CUDA builds for the models RHOAI supports — and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models the productized ROCm serving runtime will support. The campaign runs against upstream vLLM ROCm builds on dedicated AMD Instinct hardware; it does not depend on any productized platform artifact.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab arrangement) — see Risks.\n- Define the validation methodology: the supported model set under test, functional pass/fail criteria per model, and performance-parity measurement baselines against the productized CUDA runtime builds.\n- Execute the functional validation: serving each supported model on vLLM ROCm builds on the AMD Instinct nodes, including OpenAI-compatible endpoint behavior.\n- Execute the performance validation: throughput and latency measurements per model, compared against the CUDA baseline data.\n- Produce the campaign report: per-model functional results, per-model performance parity data, and the go/no-go recommendation listing the models cleared for productization.\n- Track upstream vLLM/ROCm image releases used during the campaign so results are reproducible.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The campaign report documents, for every model in the supported model set, a functional pass/fail result from serving that model on a vLLM ROCm build on AMD Instinct hardware.\n- The campaign report documents, for every functionally passing model, performance measurements (throughput and latency) with the comparison baseline and parity assessment against the CUDA runtime builds.\n- The campaign report states a go/no-go recommendation with an explicit list of models cleared for productization.\n- Every build tested is identified by a pinned upstream image reference recorded in the report.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)**: A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization. This epic is the full delivery of HLR-1.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved and controlled by parties outside this strategy. Access being granted is an availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion — the ACs above sit on the report the campaign produces. The epic's work includes driving the access arrangement to completion.\n- **Upstream cadence (open question 2)**: upstream ROCm image releases are controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; mid-campaign image updates may force re-runs. Pinned image references in the report mitigate reproducibility risk.\n- The go/no-go scope decision may require product-owner sign-off beyond the measured data; the campaign delivers the recommendation, and the sign-off is the gating human step for downstream productization.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35839,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cd51f53f58a6d98","agent_id":"ae6b38f613648449c","session_id":"ca871b20-94d5-42d0-a66d