{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e089958c2ecdb53","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM serving parity\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM serving parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis epic delivers the first-hand functional and performance validation of vLLM serving on physical AMD Instinct accelerators, covering the platform's supported model set. It resolves the strategy's central unknown — whether ROCm builds of vLLM reach functional and performance parity with the productized CUDA builds for the models RHOAI supports — and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models the productized ROCm serving runtime will support. The campaign runs against upstream vLLM ROCm builds on dedicated AMD Instinct hardware; it does not depend on any productized platform artifact.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab arrangement) — see Risks.\n34\t- Define the validation methodology: the supported model set under test, functional pass/fail criteria per model, and performance-parity measurement baselines against the productized CUDA runtime builds.\n35\t- Execute the functional validation: serving each supported model on vLLM ROCm builds on the AMD Instinct nodes, including OpenAI-compatible endpoint behavior.\n36\t- Execute the performance validation: throughput and latency measurements per model, compared against the CUDA baseline data.\n37\t- Produce the campaign report: per-model functional results, per-model performance parity data, and the go/no-go recommendation listing the models cleared for productization.\n38\t- Track upstream vLLM/ROCm image releases used during the campaign so results are reproducible.\n39\t\n40\t## Acceptance Criteria\n41\t\n42\t- The campaign report documents, for every model in the supported model set, a functional pass/fail result from serving that model on a vLLM ROCm build on AMD Instinct hardware.\n43\t- The campaign report documents, for every functionally passing model, performance measurements (throughput and latency) with the comparison baseline and parity assessment against the CUDA runtime builds.\n44\t- The campaign report states a go/no-go recommendation with an explicit list of models cleared for productization.\n45\t- Every build tested is identified by a pinned upstream image reference recorded in the report.\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability\n48\t\n49\t- **HLR-1 (P0)**: A validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization. This epic is the full delivery of HLR-1.\n50\t\n51\t## Risks / Notes\n52\t\n53\t- **Hardware access (open question 1)**: dedicated AMD Instinct nodes do not exist in the CI fleet; procurement or partner-lab access is unresolved and controlled by parties outside this strategy. Access being granted is an availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion — the ACs above sit on the report the campaign produces. The epic's work includes driving the access arrangement to completion.\n54\t- **Upstream cadence (open question 2)**: upstream ROCm image releases are controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; mid-campaign image updates may force re-runs. Pinned image references in the report mitigate reproducibility risk.\n55\t- The go/no-go scope decision may require product-owner sign-off beyond the measured data; the campaign delivers the recommendation, and the sign-off is the gating human step for downstream productization.\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88f49970e427ed12","agent_id":"a233ed0a79fbe8521","session_id":"ca871b20-94d5-42d0-a66d-2838b98e5410","uuid":"6e060958-793a-4d1e-9bfa-160cbfe57982","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:28:25.873Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e089958c2ecdb53","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
