{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5fdb22b7935a23a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E002\n3\ttitle: Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the campaign clears fewer models than the supported set, the\n15\t    productized runtime's supported-model scope shrinks to the cleared list; a total\n16\t    no-go removes productization of the ROCm serving runtime.\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 0\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: -1\n23\t  external_dependency: -1\n24\t  human_process_gates: -1\n25\t  repo_access: 1\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: Medium\n30\tai_implementability_score: 2\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime for validated models\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tThis epic delivers a supported, productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime for the models the AMD Instinct validation campaign clears. It builds a ROCm variant of the vLLM runtime image from the existing midstream vLLM repo, defines the ServingRuntime/ClusterServingRuntime resources that deploy it on AMD GPU nodes, and brings the runtime through the platform's release and support process so it ships per platform version alongside the CUDA builds. The scope of supported models is determined by the campaign's go/no-go outcome; this epic starts once that outcome is known.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Build the vLLM ROCm runtime image variant in the existing midstream vLLM repo, based on the upstream ROCm builds validated by the campaign.\n43\t- Define the ServingRuntime (or ClusterServingRuntime) resource that schedules the ROCm runtime on AMD GPU nodes, mirroring the existing CUDA runtime resources.\n44\t- Restrict the supported/validated model surface to the models cleared by the validation campaign's go/no-go scope.\n45\t- Validate serving through KServe InferenceServices using the ROCm runtime (passive-component validation: KServe itself requires no code change — endpoint readiness and request serving must be demonstrated through the standard InferenceService path).\n46\t- Align the release with an upstream ROCm base image that is available and supportable for the target platform version (see Risks).\n47\t- Provide rollout/rollback safety: the CUDA runtime builds remain the default; the ROCm runtime is selectable without displacing existing deployments.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- A vLLM ROCm runtime image is built from the midstream repo and released with a platform-version-pinned reference.\n52\t- A ServingRuntime resource deploying the ROCm image is available and, when selected, serves models on AMD GPU nodes through KServe InferenceServices with endpoints reporting Ready.\n53\t- Every model listed as supported by this runtime is on the validated list produced by the AMD Instinct validation campaign, and that mapping is recorded.\n54\t- Serving of at least the cleared model set is demonstrated end-to-end on AMD Instinct hardware through the InferenceService path.\n55\t- The build pipeline for the ROCm image is green through the Konflux pipeline (Rule 25 coupling — this epic does not wait on the pipeline-onboarding work as a dependency; the green pipeline is a completion condition of this epic's release).\n56\t- Existing CUDA-based serving runtimes continue to deploy and serve unchanged when the ROCm runtime is introduced (rollback/default-behavior safety).\n57\t- The release uses an upstream ROCm base image whose availability aligns with the platform release train, or the misalignment is escalated and recorded with a mitigation.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- **HLR-2 (P0)**: A supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime is productized for the models the validation campaign clears. This epic is the full delivery of HLR-2, gated on HLR-1's campaign outcome for its model scope.\n62\t\n63\t## Risks / Notes\n64\t\n65\t- **Upstream ROCm build cadence (open question 2)**: AMD's runtime image release timing is outside our control and may not align with platform release trains; a misalignment is a release-planning escalation, not a code change.\n66\t- **Gate**: this epic's model scope is rewritten by the validation campaign's go/no-go outcome — a narrower cleared list narrows the supported model surface; a total no-go would remove the epic.\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88f49970e427ed12","agent_id":"a233ed0a79fbe8521","session_id":"ca871b20-94d5-42d0-a66d-2838b98e5410","uuid":"2e6d562d-817a-4923-bb85-d29b2edf7d3b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:28:26.491Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5fdb22b7935a23a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
