{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfed0a3254557b48","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n9\t  description: E002 pipeline-green AC and E006 HLR Traceability cite internal decomposition\n10\t    methodology (\"Rule 25\", \"docs-authoring exception\") that is meaningless in Jira;\n11\t    strip rule citations from epic bodies, keeping the testable statement\n12\terror: null\n13\t---\n14\t## Review Summary\n15\t\n16\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n17\tRecommendation: accept\n18\t\n19\tContract validator: OK (exit 0) — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11\n20\tdocs-last coverage, priority inheritance, Rules 7/8/10 implementation-type parallelism, and\n21\tshared-surface enforcement deliverables all pass. Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full\n22\tdecomposition), so the 6-epic multi-stream shape is expected and correct.\n23\t\n24\tThe decomposition is a clean translation of the strategy. All four HLRs map to epics with a\n25\tcomplete traceability matrix; the DAG matches frontmatter dependencies exactly (E001→E002,\n26\tE002/E003/E004/E005→E006) with arrow direction in the dependency→dependent convention; the\n27\tkonflux-onboarding epic (E003) is correctly kept parallel to the general implementation epic\n28\t(E002) with the coupling expressed as the Rule 25 \"pipeline green\" AC rather than a DAG edge;\n29\tthe Investigation epic genuinely gates E002 with a properly populated `gate_failure_impact`;\n30\thardware access and upstream cadence are handled as risks/tiered delivery rather than\n31\tmisfiled ACs or DAG edges; and the `amd.com/gpu` exposure gap is correctly flagged for human\n32\tverification instead of invented. One minor style nit (below) does not affect the score.\n33\t\n34\t## Criterion Details\n35\t\n36\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n37\t